  "prefLabel": "Ph 2301.07 Drug Control In Ambulatory Patient Treatment Areas",
  "recordType": "rule-section",
  "ruleText": "Ph 2301.07 Drug Control In Ambulatory Patient Treatment Areas.\n\n(a) If a licensed pharmacist is on the premises, that pharmacist may fill one-time, full amount, non-refillable prescriptions for patients for medications related to the ambulatory patient treatment visit.\n\n(b) In the ambulatory patient treatment areas, a practitioner may dispense a maximum of a 72-hour supply of drugs for the immediate needs of the patient, subject to the following exceptions:\n\n(1) For schedule II-IV controlled substances, no more than a 48-hour supply may be dispensed;\n\n(2) Drugs typically dispensed in multi-dose form including but not limited to inhalers, epi-pens, creams, and ointments, shall be dispensed as packaged;\n\n(3) A full course of medication therapy needed to treat patients under a public health incident may be dispensed;\n\n(4) A full course of medication therapy needed to treat patients of sexual assault may be dispensed; and\n\n(5) Drugs related to opioid overdose prevention, such as naloxone, may be dispensed for risk mitigation with an exemption from labeling requirements that identify the individual patient.\n\n(c) A readily retrievable record shall be made of all controlled drugs dispensed in the ambulatory patient area that contains the following:\n\n(1) Name and address of the patient;\n\n(2) Name of the practitioner;\n\n(3) Name, strength, and quantity of each drug dispensed;\n\n(4) Date of administration or dispensing; and\n\n(5) Signature or electronic identifier, as defined in Ph 2301.0l(d), of the practitioner’s agent removing the drug(s) from the inventory.\n\nSource. #13323, eff 1-5-22; ss by #13324, EMERGENCY RULE, eff 1-5-22, EXPIRED: 7-4-22; reverted back to #13323; ss by #13436, eff 8-18-22; ss by #14592, eff 6-1-26, EXPIRES: 6-1-36",
